---
// The guide: succession explained well, with worked examples the tests check, sources, and the
// day it was last brought up to date. Search engines get the breadcrumb and the article's date.
import { article, ref, wikipedia } from '@eurth/style/build';
import Page from '@eurth/style/components/Page.astro';
import { site } from '../../eurth.config.mjs';
import Worked from '../components/Worked.astro';
import { workedById } from '../content/worked';

const updated = '2026-11-15';
const headline = 'How royal succession works';
const description = 'Five succession laws explained with worked family trees: primogeniture, the male line, seniority and election, plus regencies, pretenders and how real houses kept their records.';
const out = (href: string) => ref(href, site.host);
const wiki = (title: string) => wikipedia(title, site.host);
---

<Page site={site} title={headline} description={description} crumbs={[{ name: 'Guide', path: '/guide/' }]} jsonld={[article(site, { headline, path: '/guide/', description, updated })]}>
  <article class="eu-prose guide">
    <p class="eu-kicker">Guide</p>
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="eu-lead">
      A succession law is a rule for answering one question before anyone has to fight over it: when the ruler dies, who is next? Every law here answers it
      differently, and every one has started a war somewhere. Pick the one whose quarrels suit your story.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each law below comes with a worked example: a small invented family, and the line of succession that <a href="/">Dynasty</a> gives for it. Open any of them
      in the tool and change it. The <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> has the vocabulary and the <a href="/examples/">examples</a> have six complete houses.
    </p>

    <nav aria-label="In this guide">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#basics">Three ideas every law shares</a></li>
        <li><a href="#absolute">Absolute primogeniture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#male-preference">Male-preference primogeniture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#agnatic">Agnatic primogeniture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#seniority">Agnatic seniority</a></li>
        <li><a href="#elective">Elective monarchy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#left-out">Who is left out</a></li>
        <li><a href="#regencies">Regencies</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pretenders">Claimants and pretenders</a></li>
        <li><a href="#records">How real houses kept their records</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="basics">Three ideas every law shares</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The line descends from someone.</strong> A line of succession is reckoned from a <a href="/glossary/#founder">founder</a>: the first of the house to
      hold the crown, or whoever the law names. Only the founder's descendants have places. The queen's sister-in-law, however formidable, does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The dead are passed over, but their children are not.</strong> If the heir dies before the ruler, the heir's children stand where their parent
      stood. This is <a href="/glossary/#representation">representation</a>, and it is why a grandchild can come before a living uncle. The alternative,
      <a href="/glossary/#proximity-of-blood">proximity of blood</a>, prefers the nearer relative, and the two rules met on a good many medieval battlefields.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('representation')} draw={false} />
    <p>
      <strong>A line runs down before it runs across.</strong> Under primogeniture, the eldest child's whole branch comes before the second child. The ruler's
      great-grandchild by the eldest ranks above the ruler's own second child. Seniority, further down, is the one law here that works the other way.
    </p>

    <h2 id="absolute">Absolute primogeniture</h2>
    <p>
      The eldest child inherits, daughter or son. It is the simplest rule there is and, for monarchies, a recent one: Sweden adopted it in 1980, which made the
      infant Princess Victoria heir ahead of her younger brother. The Netherlands, Norway, Belgium, Denmark and Luxembourg followed, and the United Kingdom did so
      with the Succession to the Crown Act 2013, for those born after 28 October 2011.
    </p>
    <p>For a writer, its drama lies elsewhere: with nothing to argue about in the rule, the arguments move to legitimacy, marriages and renunciations.</p>
    <Worked example={workedById('absolute')} />

    <h2 id="male-preference">Male-preference primogeniture</h2>
    <p>
      Sons before daughters, but daughters before uncles. A woman inherits when she has no living brothers and no dead brother left heirs. This was the rule of
      England and then Britain for centuries, and it is why Elizabeth II reigned: her father had two daughters and no sons. Spain still uses it (Article 57 of the
      1978 constitution), and so does Monaco.
    </p>
    <p>
      It produces a particular kind of household: an eldest daughter who is heir until the day a brother is born. In the vocabulary of succession she is an
      <a href="/glossary/#heir-presumptive">heir presumptive</a>, never an heir apparent.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('male-preference')} />

    <h2 id="agnatic">Agnatic primogeniture</h2>
    <p>
      Men descended through men, and nobody else. A daughter cannot inherit, and neither can her sons: the claim cannot pass through her. It is often called
      <a href="/glossary/#salic-law">Salic law</a>, after a Frankish code that French lawyers dusted off in the fourteenth century. In 1316 and again in 1328 the
      French crown passed over a king's daughter and her line; in 1328 the passed-over claimant was Edward III of England, whose mother was a French princess. The
      Hundred Years' War followed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Japan's Imperial Household Law of 1947 keeps the rule today: the throne passes to male offspring in the male line. So did Hanover, which is why it parted
      company with Britain in 1837, when Victoria took one throne and her uncle the other.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('agnatic')} />
    <p>
      The classic crisis of an agnatic house is a ruler with daughters only. He can accept that a nephew or cousin will follow him, or he can try to change the
      law. The Emperor Charles VI spent decades persuading Europe to accept his <a href={wiki('Pragmatic Sanction of 1713')}>Pragmatic Sanction of 1713</a>, so
      that his daughter Maria Theresa could inherit the Habsburg lands. He died in 1740, and the War of the Austrian Succession began within the year.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('only-daughters')} />

    <h2 id="seniority">Agnatic seniority</h2>
    <p>
      Still the male line, but the crown moves sideways before it moves down: from a ruler to his brothers, eldest first, and to the next generation only when the
      older one is used up. The sons of Yaroslav the Wise shared out Kievan Rus this way after 1054; the Ottoman throne went to the eldest living male of the
      dynasty from 1617; and the Saudi throne has passed from brother to brother among the sons of Ibn Saud since 1953.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its strength is that the ruler is always a grown man. Its weaknesses are short reigns by elderly brothers, and a crowd of cousins who each stand a few
      funerals from the throne.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('seniority')} />
    <Worked example={workedById('brothers-first')} />

    <h2 id="elective">Elective monarchy</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody inherits. When the ruler dies, a body of <a href="/glossary/#elector">electors</a> chooses the next one, usually for life. The Golden Bull of 1356
      fixed seven prince-electors for the Holy Roman Empire. In Poland-Lithuania, from 1573, every nobleman could vote, and tens of thousands turned up in a field
      outside Warsaw to do so. Malaysia's nine hereditary rulers still elect one of their number as head of state for five years at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Election does not keep families out. The Habsburgs were elected emperor almost without a break from 1438 to 1740: an elective crown can be hereditary in
      everything but law, so long as the electors are kept content. In Dynasty, an elective law makes the line whatever you say it is: you rank the candidates, and
      blood promises nothing.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('elective')} />

    <h2 id="left-out">Who is left out</h2>
    <p>The law gives the order. The house rules decide who is in the queue at all. Dynasty has a switch for each, because real houses disagreed about every one.</p>
    <h3>Born outside marriage</h3>
    <p>
      Most hereditary systems gave a child born outside marriage no claim. Most, not all: William the Conqueror was known in his lifetime as William the Bastard,
      and inherited Normandy in 1035 all the same. A child could also be <a href="/glossary/#legitimation">legitimised</a> later. John of Gaunt's Beaufort children
      were, in 1397, and a clause added in 1407 barred them from the throne anyway. The Tudor claim ran through them regardless.
    </p>
    <h3>Renunciation and abdication</h3>
    <p>
      A <a href="/glossary/#renunciation">renunciation</a> gives up a place in the line, for oneself or for one's descendants too. Philip V of Spain renounced the
      French throne for himself and his line as the price of keeping the Spanish one, in the settlement that ended the War of the Spanish Succession. An
      <a href="/glossary/#abdication">abdication</a> is different: a reigning monarch steps down, the heir succeeds as if the monarch had died, and the monarch's
      children keep their places. Edward VIII abdicated in 1936; Queen Beatrix of the Netherlands in 2013, in favour of her son.
    </p>
    <h3>Marrying out</h3>
    <p>
      Many houses demanded that their members marry equals, or marry with permission. Archduke Franz Ferdinand married Countess Sophie Chotek in 1900 only after
      swearing that their children would never succeed: a <a href="/glossary/#morganatic-marriage">morganatic marriage</a>. In the Netherlands, a member of the
      royal house who marries without parliament's consent loses their place, as Prince Friso did in 2004. In Dynasty, a marriage “out” costs the person their
      place and the children of that marriage theirs; children of an earlier, recognised marriage are untouched.
    </p>
    <h3>Adoption</h3>
    <p>
      European houses almost never let an adopted child inherit a throne. Rome did little else for a century: Nerva adopted Trajan, who adopted Hadrian, who adopted
      Antoninus Pius, who adopted Marcus Aurelius. Dynasty leaves adopted children out unless you switch that rule off.
    </p>
    <Worked example={workedById('exclusions')} />
    <Worked example={workedById('illegitimacy-off')} draw={false} />
    <Worked example={workedById('renounce-self')} draw={false} />
    <Worked example={workedById('adoption-on')} draw={false} />

    <h2 id="regencies">Regencies</h2>
    <p>
      Primogeniture has a price: sooner or later it crowns a child. Louis XIV became king at four, Louis XV at five. A <a href="/glossary/#regency">regency</a> is
      the answer: the child reigns, and a <a href="/glossary/#regent">regent</a> governs until the child comes of age. The same is done for a monarch who is ill or
      abroad, as when the future George IV ruled as Prince Regent from 1811 during his father's last illness.
    </p>
    <p>
      Who the regent is matters as much as who the heir is. The usual candidates are the monarch's mother (Anne of Austria for Louis XIV), the nearest adult in the
      line (the rule of Britain's Regency Act 1937) or a council. Each has an interest of their own, and the nearest adult in the line is, by definition, the
      person who gains most if the child does not grow up. Storytellers have noticed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dynasty does not appoint regents, but it tells you when you need one: give people birth years, and a reigning child is plain to see. Record the regent in the
      child's notes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pretenders">Claimants and pretenders</h2>
    <p>
      A <a href="/glossary/#claimant">claimant</a> says the throne should be theirs. A <a href="/glossary/#pretender">pretender</a> is a claimant to a throne that
      someone else holds, or that no longer exists; the word comes from the French for “claimant” and makes no comment on the claim. They arise in three ways.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Two laws, one throne.</strong> When Ferdinand VII of Spain set aside the Salic rule in 1830 so that his daughter Isabella could succeed, his brother
        Carlos claimed the crown under the old law. Their followers, the Carlists, fought three wars over it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A ruler removed.</strong> James II was driven from Britain in 1688. His son and grandson, the Old and Young Pretenders, kept the Jacobite claim
        alive for two generations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A throne abolished.</strong> Republican France has had three royal and imperial lines waiting at once: the Legitimists, the Orléanists and the
        Bonapartists, each with its own law of succession.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To build a pretender in Dynasty, draw the family once and ask it two questions. Switch the law, or change who the line descends from, and compare the heirs:
      wherever the answers differ, you have a rival court. What if does this for you when a ruler dies, naming who would reign under each other law.
    </p>

    <h2 id="records">How real houses kept their records</h2>
    <p>
      A claim is only as good as the record of it, so ruling families were early and careful genealogists. Medieval houses kept pedigree rolls, long parchments
      with the founder at the top. In England, from 1530 to 1688, the heralds toured the counties on
      <a href={wiki('Heraldic visitation')}>visitations</a>, recording who was entitled to arms and from whom they descended; the
      <a href={out('https://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/')}>College of Arms</a> still holds the books.
    </p>
    <p>
      From 1763 to 1944 the <a href={wiki('Almanach de Gotha')}>Almanach de Gotha</a> listed Europe's reigning and formerly reigning houses every year, and being
      in the right section of it settled arguments about who could marry whom. Burke's Peerage has done the same for British titled families since 1826. Many
      German houses wrote their own <a href="/glossary/#house-law">house laws</a>, and Japan's Imperial Household keeps the Kōtōfu, the register of the imperial
      line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Genealogists gave us two tools worth borrowing. The <a href="/glossary/#ahnentafel">ahnentafel</a>, first printed in 1590, numbers a person's ancestors so
      that any ancestor's father is twice their number. And <a href="/glossary/#gedcom">GEDCOM</a>, since 1984, is the plain-text file that lets one family tree
      program read another's work; Dynasty reads and writes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open the <a href="/">tree</a>, or start from one of the <a href="/examples/">examples</a>. When your house has an heir, a spare and a grievance, give it a
      country: <a href="https://eurth.org">Eurth</a> has a world for it to live in.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={wiki('Order of succession')}>Order of succession</a>, <a href={wiki('Primogeniture')}>Primogeniture</a> and <a href={wiki('Salic law')}>Salic law</a>, Wikipedia.</li>
      <li><a href={wiki('Agnatic seniority')}>Agnatic seniority</a> and <a href={wiki('Elective monarchy')}>Elective monarchy</a>, Wikipedia.</li>
      <li><a href={wiki('Morganatic marriage')}>Morganatic marriage</a>, <a href={wiki('Regent')}>Regent</a> and <a href={wiki('Pretender')}>Pretender</a>, Wikipedia.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2013/20/contents')}>Succession to the Crown Act 2013</a>, legislation.gov.uk: the United Kingdom's move to absolute primogeniture.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Edw8and1Geo6/1/16/contents')}>Regency Act 1937</a>, legislation.gov.uk: who is regent, and when.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-1978-31229')}>Constitución Española</a>, Boletín Oficial del Estado: Article 57 sets out male-preference primogeniture.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://www.kunaicho.go.jp/e-kunaicho/hourei-01.html')}>The Imperial House Law</a>, Imperial Household Agency of Japan, in English.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://avalon.law.yale.edu/medieval/golden.asp')}>The Golden Bull of the Emperor Charles IV, 1356</a>, the Avalon Project, Yale Law School.</li>
      <li><a href={wiki('Almanach de Gotha')}>Almanach de Gotha</a>, <a href={wiki('Heraldic visitation')}>Heraldic visitation</a> and <a href={wiki('Ahnentafel')}>Ahnentafel</a>, Wikipedia.</li>
      <li><a href={out('https://gedcom.io/specs/')}>The GEDCOM specifications</a>, FamilySearch.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="eu-muted">Last updated <time datetime={updated}>15 November 2026</time>.</p>
  </article>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Headings that a link jumps to stay clear of the top of the window. */
  .guide :is(h2, h3) {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  /* The contents list: each link is a line of its own, so each is tall enough to press. */
  .guide nav ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .guide nav a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--size-target);
  }
</style>
